ers or edges to the opponent.\n\nEvaluation: \nThe valid moves are (B, 7), (C, 7), (D, 7), (E, 7), (F, 7), (G, 5), (G, 6), (H, 2), (H, 4).\n- Moves (B, 7), (G, 6), and (H, 2) involve X-squares or C-squares (squares adjacent to corners), which are strategically dangerous as they often allow the opponent to take the corner.\n- Move (H, 4) is an edge move that flips two pieces, expanding the frontier significantly and potentially allowing the opponent to stabilize the edge.\n- Moves (D, 7) and (E, 7) are the most central and stable options. Both flip only one piece, minimizing the new frontier exposed to the opponent.\n\nReason: \nComparing (D, 7) and (E, 7):\n- Playing (D, 7) flips D6, changing the row 6 pattern to `* @ *`. This fragments the opponent\'s pieces.\n- Playing (E, 7) flips E6, changing the row 6 pattern to `* * @`. This maintains a contiguous block of opponent pieces at C6 and D6.\nKeeping the opponent\'s pieces clustered is generally superior because it provides a solid target for future moves and restricts the opponent\'s mobility. Additionally, flipping E6 (turning it into `@`) disconnects it from the opponent\'s cluster at F5, weakening their local structure. (E, 7) is a safe, central move that improves my influence on the E-column and limits the opponent\'s responses.\n\nAnswer: (E, 7)'}
