tegy in the long run.\n\n\n", 'output': "Situation: The game is in the mid-to-late stage. I am playing as '@' and currently hold fewer discs than the opponent '*'. The opponent has control over much of the center and parts of the right edge. However, I have a valid move at the top-left corner (A, 1).\n\nEvaluation:\n1.  **(A, 1)**: This is a corner move. Gaining a corner is the most strategic objective in Reversi as it provides a stable disc that can never be flipped. It also allows for future edge expansion. The move flips the diagonal pieces at B2 and C3, utilizing my piece at D4 as the anchor.\n2.  **(B, 1)** and **(G, 1)**: These are C-squares (squares adjacent to corners). Playing these typically allows the opponent to capture the adjacent corners (A1 or H1), making them poor strategic choices unless forced.\n3.  **(F, 6)**: This is a strong internal move that would flip many opponent pieces (along column 6, row F, and the diagonal). While it gains material, simply flipping pieces is often less valuable than securing structural advantages like corners.\n4.  **(D, 8)** and **(E, 8)**: These are edge moves. While reasonable, they do not offer the permanent stability of a corner.\n\nReason: The move at (A, 1) secures a corner, which is the most critical strategic asset in the game. It creates a permanent anchor for my pieces and prevents the opponent from playing there. It is superior to all other available moves, particularly the dangerous C-square moves or simple high-flip moves that do not secure territory.\n\nAnswer: (A, 1)"}
